/* Go to heaven · Biology question bank (IB Biology, first assessment 2025). */
QB("bio", [
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain why water is a good solvent.","Water molecules are polar|polar|polarity|dipole","So they form hydrogen bonds / attractions with ions and other polar molecules, dissolving them|hydrogen bond|attract|ions|charged|dissolv"],
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why water has a high specific heat capacity.","There are many hydrogen bonds between water molecules|hydrogen bond","A lot of energy is needed to break them, so its temperature changes slowly (stable habitats)|lot of energy|much energy|break|slowly|stable|resist"],
["A1.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between DNA and RNA.","DNA contains deoxyribose; RNA contains ribose|deoxyribose|ribose","DNA has thymine; RNA has uracil|thymine|uracil","DNA is double-stranded; RNA is single-stranded|double|single strand|strand"],
["A1.2","State",2,"State the complementary base pairs in DNA, using full names.","Adenine pairs with thymine|adenine thymine|thymine adenine","Cytosine pairs with guanine|cytosine guanine|guanine cytosine"],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the endosymbiotic theory.","Mitochondria and chloroplasts were once free-living prokaryotes|prokaryot|bacteria|free living","That were engulfed by a larger cell (by endocytosis) and became organelles|engulf|endocytosis|taken in|absorbed|swallowed"],
["A2.1","State",2,"State two pieces of evidence for endosymbiosis.","They have their own circular DNA|circular dna|own dna|naked dna","They have 70S ribosomes|70s|ribosome","They have a double membrane|double membrane|two membranes","They divide by binary fission|binary fission|divide"],
["A2.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotes have no nucleus; eukaryotes have one|nucleus","Prokaryotes have no membrane-bound organelles|membrane bound|organelles","Prokaryotes have 70S ribosomes; eukaryotes have 80S|70s|80s","Prokaryotic DNA is circular and naked; eukaryotic DNA is linear with histones|circular|naked|linear|histone"],
["A2.2","State",2,"State two structures found in plant cells but not in animal cells.","Cell wall|cell wall|cellulose","Chloroplasts|chloroplast","Large permanent vacuole|vacuole"],
["A2.3","Outline",2,"Outline why viruses are not usually considered living.","They have no cell structure or metabolism of their own|no cell|not cell|acellular|no metabolism|no cytoplasm","They can only reproduce inside a host cell|host|inside a cell|only reproduce|need a cell"],
["A2.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between the lytic and lysogenic cycles.","Lytic: the virus replicates and bursts (lyses) the host cell|burst|lys|destroy|kill","Lysogenic: viral DNA is inserted into the host genome and stays dormant|insert|integrat|genome|dormant|latent|prophage"],
["A3.1","Define",2,"Define species, using the biological species concept.","A group of organisms that can interbreed|interbreed|breed together|mate","To produce fertile offspring|fertile offspring|fertile"],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the binomial system of naming.","Two names: the genus then the species|genus|two names|two part","Genus starts with a capital, species is lower case, written in italics (underlined by hand)|capital|italic|underline|lower case|latin"],
["A3.2","Define",2,"Define a clade.","A group of organisms with a common ancestor|common ancestor|ancestor","Including all of its descendants|all descendants|all of its descendants|descendant"],
["A3.2","State",3,"State the three domains of life.","Bacteria|bacteria","Archaea|archaea","Eukarya / eukaryotes|eukary"],
["A4.1","Outline",3,"Outline allopatric speciation.","A population is split by a geographical barrier|geographic|barrier|separated|isolated","The groups face different selection pressures / mutations and evolve differently|different selection|different environment|evolve|mutation","Until they can no longer interbreed (reproductive isolation), forming new species|no longer interbreed|cannot interbreed|reproductive isolation|new species"],
["A4.1","State",2,"State two types of evidence for evolution.","The fossil record|fossil","DNA or protein sequence comparisons|dna|sequence|molecular|protein","Homologous structures / comparative anatomy|homologous|anatomy|pentadactyl","Selective (artificial) breeding|selective breeding|artificial selection","Observed changes, e.g. antibiotic resistance|antibiotic resistance|peppered moth|observed"],
["A4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between in situ and ex situ conservation.","In situ: protecting species in their natural habitat, e.g. nature reserves|natural habitat|nature reserve|national park|in the wild","Ex situ: conserving species outside their habitat, e.g. zoos and seed banks|outside|zoo|seed bank|captive|botanic"],
["A4.2","State",2,"State two causes of biodiversity loss.","Habitat destruction / deforestation|habitat|deforest","Pollution|pollut","Climate change|climate","Overexploitation (overhunting, overfishing)|overexploit|overhunt|overfish|poach","Invasive species|invasive|alien species"],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline how a disaccharide is formed.","Two monosaccharides join|two monosaccharides|two sugars|monosaccharide","By a condensation reaction, forming a glycosidic bond and releasing water|condensation|glycosidic|water"],
["B1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.","Saturated fatty acids have only single C–C bonds|single bond|no double","Unsaturated fatty acids have one or more C=C double bonds (kinks)|double bond|kink"],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the primary and secondary structure of a protein.","Primary: the sequence of amino acids|sequence|order of amino acids","Secondary: α-helices and β-pleated sheets held by hydrogen bonds|helix|helices|pleated|sheet|hydrogen bond"],
["B1.2","Define",2,"Define denaturation.","A change in a protein's 3D shape|shape|3d|structure","Caused by heat or pH, so it loses its function|heat|temperature|ph|lose function|no longer"],
["B2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between active transport and facilitated diffusion.","Active transport uses ATP and moves substances against the gradient|atp|energy|against","Facilitated diffusion uses no ATP and moves down the gradient through channel or carrier proteins|no energy|passive|down|channel|carrier"],
["B2.1","Define",2,"Define osmosis.","The net movement of water|water","Across a partially permeable membrane, from lower to higher solute concentration|partially permeable|semi permeable|membrane|higher solute|lower solute|water potential"],
["B2.2","State",2,"State the function of mitochondria and of ribosomes.","Mitochondria: site of aerobic respiration, producing ATP|respiration|atp|energy","Ribosomes: site of protein synthesis|protein synthesis|make protein|translation|proteins"],
["B2.2","Explain",2,"Explain one advantage of compartmentalization in eukaryotic cells.","Harmful or conflicting reactions and enzymes are kept apart, e.g. digestive enzymes in lysosomes|separate|lysosome|digestive enzymes|harmful|conflicting|apart","Each compartment can keep ideal conditions or concentrations for its reactions|ideal|optimal|concentration|efficient|specific conditions"],
["B2.3","Define",2,"Define stem cell.","An unspecialized (undifferentiated) cell|unspecialis|unspecializ|undifferentiat","That can divide and differentiate into specialized cell types|differentiat|specialised|specialized|divide|self renew"],
["B2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why cell size is limited by the surface area to volume ratio.","As a cell grows, its volume increases faster than its surface area|volume increases faster|ratio decreases|ratio falls|smaller ratio","So exchange across the membrane can no longer meet the cell's needs|exchange|diffusion|not enough|cannot supply|remove waste"],
["B3.1","State",2,"State two adaptations of alveoli for gas exchange.","Large surface area|surface area","Thin walls, one cell thick|thin|one cell","Moist lining|moist","Dense capillary network / good blood supply|capillar|blood supply","Surfactant|surfactant"],
["B3.1","Outline",3,"Outline the process of inhalation.","The diaphragm contracts and flattens|diaphragm","The external intercostal muscles contract, raising the ribs|intercostal|ribs","The volume of the thorax increases and pressure falls, so air flows in|volume increase|pressure decrease|pressure fall|lower pressure|air flows in|air in"],
["B3.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between arteries and veins.","Arteries have thick, muscular, elastic walls; veins have thin walls|thick|thin|muscular|elastic","Arteries have a narrow lumen; veins have a wide lumen|lumen","Veins have valves; arteries do not|valve","Arteries carry blood at high pressure away from the heart|high pressure|away from the heart"],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how water moves up the xylem.","Transpiration from the leaves creates tension that pulls water up|transpiration|tension|pull|suction","Cohesion between water molecules and adhesion to the xylem walls|cohesion|adhesion|hydrogen bond"],
["B3.3","Outline",3,"Outline the sliding filament model of muscle contraction.","Myosin heads bind to actin, forming cross-bridges|myosin|actin|cross bridge","ATP is used to pull the actin filaments towards the centre (power stroke)|atp|power stroke|pull","The sarcomere shortens|sarcomere|shorten"],
["B3.3","State",2,"State the role of calcium ions in muscle contraction.","They are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum|sarcoplasmic reticulum","They bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin to expose the binding sites on actin|troponin|tropomyosin|binding site|expose"],
["B4.1","State",2,"State two adaptations of desert plants (xerophytes).","Thick waxy cuticle|wax|cuticle","Few or sunken stomata|stomata","Small leaves or spines|spine|small leaves|needle","Deep or wide roots|root","Water storage tissue|store water|succulent"],
["B4.1","Define",2,"Define habitat.","The place where an organism lives|place|where|lives|location","Including its environmental conditions|conditions|environment|abiotic"],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline the competitive exclusion principle.","Two species cannot occupy the same niche|same niche|identical niche","In the same place for long: one outcompetes the other, which dies out or moves|outcompete|die out|extinct|excluded|one wins"],
["B4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between fundamental and realized niches.","Fundamental niche: the full range of conditions a species could occupy|could|potential|full range|without competition","Realized niche: the part it actually occupies because of competition|actually|competition|smaller|limited"],
["C1.1","Explain",3,"Explain the effect of high temperature on enzyme activity.","Activity rises up to the optimum as molecules move faster and collide more|optimum|collision|collide|kinetic energy|faster","Above the optimum, bonds holding the enzyme's shape break|bonds break|hydrogen bonds|vibrat","The active site changes shape, the enzyme is denatured and activity falls|denature|active site|shape"],
["C1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between competitive and non-competitive inhibition.","Competitive inhibitors bind to the active site|active site","Non-competitive inhibitors bind elsewhere (an allosteric site), changing the active site's shape|allosteric|elsewhere|another site|different site|change shape"],
["C1.2","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between aerobic and anaerobic respiration in humans.","Aerobic respiration needs oxygen; anaerobic does not|oxygen","Aerobic produces carbon dioxide and water; anaerobic produces lactate|carbon dioxide|water|lactate|lactic acid","Aerobic yields far more ATP|more atp|36|38|2 atp"],
["C1.2","State",2,"State where glycolysis and the Krebs cycle take place.","Glycolysis: the cytoplasm|cytoplasm","Krebs cycle: the mitochondrial matrix|matrix|mitochondri"],
["C1.3","State",2,"State the word equation for photosynthesis.","Carbon dioxide + water (with light energy)|carbon dioxide water|co2 water|water carbon dioxide","→ glucose + oxygen|glucose oxygen|sugar oxygen|oxygen glucose"],
["C1.3","State",2,"State two limiting factors of photosynthesis.","Light intensity|light","Carbon dioxide concentration|carbon dioxide|co2","Temperature|temperature"],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline how a hormone reaches and affects its target cells.","It is secreted by a gland into the blood|blood|gland|secret","It binds to specific receptors on or in its target cells|receptor|target|specific"],
["C2.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between how steroid and peptide hormones act.","Steroid hormones pass through the membrane and bind to receptors inside the cell|through the membrane|inside|intracellular|nucleus|lipid soluble","Peptide hormones bind to receptors on the cell surface and act through second messengers|surface|membrane receptor|second messenger|camp"],
["C2.2","Outline",3,"Outline how a nerve impulse passes across a synapse.","The impulse causes neurotransmitter to be released from vesicles|neurotransmitter|vesicle|release","The neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft|diffuse|cleft|gap","It binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, starting a new impulse|receptor|postsynaptic|bind"],
["C2.2","Define",2,"Define resting potential.","The potential difference across a neuron's membrane when it is not transmitting|potential difference|membrane|at rest|not transmitting","About −70 mV, maintained by the sodium–potassium pump|70|sodium potassium|pump"],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the roles of the nervous and endocrine systems in coordinating the body.","Nervous system: fast, short-lived electrical impulses along neurons|nerve|neuron|electrical|fast|impulse","Endocrine system: slower, longer-lasting hormones carried in the blood|hormone|blood|slow|longer"],
["C3.1","Define",1,"Define reflex.","A rapid, involuntary response to a stimulus|involuntary|automatic|without thinking|unconscious"],
["C3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between innate and adaptive immunity.","Innate immunity is non-specific and the same for all pathogens (e.g. skin, phagocytes)|non specific|nonspecific|same|phagocyte|skin|general","Adaptive immunity is specific to one pathogen, uses lymphocytes and antibodies, and has memory|specific|lymphocyte|antibod|memory"],
["C3.2","Explain",2,"Explain how vaccination gives immunity.","The vaccine contains antigens from a weakened or dead pathogen|antigen|weakened|dead|inactiv","It triggers memory cells, so a later infection gets a fast, strong response|memory cell|memory|faster response|secondary response"],
["C4.1","State",2,"State two density-dependent factors that limit population growth.","Competition for food or space|competition|food","Predation|predat","Disease and parasites|disease|parasite"],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how capture–mark–release–recapture estimates the size of an animal population.","A first sample is caught, marked harmlessly and released to mix back in|harmless|harmlessly|paint|tag|tagged|mix|first sample","A second sample is caught later, and population = (first × second) ÷ number of marked animals recaptured|second sample|lincoln|multiply|multiplied|divide|divided|×|÷"],
["C4.2","Explain",2,"Explain why food chains rarely have more than five trophic levels.","Energy is lost at each level, mostly as heat from respiration and in waste|energy lost|heat|respiration|waste|10","So too little energy is left to support higher levels|not enough|too little|insufficient"],
["C4.2","State",2,"State two processes that release carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","Respiration|respiration","Combustion / burning fossil fuels|combust|burn","Decomposition|decompos","Volcanic eruptions|volcan"],
["D1.1","Define",2,"Define semi-conservative replication.","Each new DNA molecule keeps one original strand|one original|original strand|parent strand|old strand","And one newly made strand|new strand|newly"],
["D1.1","State",2,"State the roles of helicase and DNA polymerase.","Helicase unwinds and separates the strands by breaking hydrogen bonds|unwind|separate|hydrogen bond|unzip","DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides to build the new strand|nucleotide|new strand|complementary|join"],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline transcription.","A DNA strand is used as a template to make mRNA|mrna|template","By RNA polymerase, in the nucleus|rna polymerase|nucleus"],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline translation.","A ribosome reads the mRNA in codons|ribosome|codon","tRNA brings specific amino acids, joined by peptide bonds into a polypeptide|trna|anticodon|amino acid|peptide bond|polypeptide"],
["D1.3","Define",2,"Define gene mutation.","A change in the base sequence|base sequence|change in dna|dna sequence|nucleotide sequence","Of a gene, which may produce a new allele|base sequence of a gene|sequence of a gene|dna of a gene|new allele|allele"],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline how CRISPR–Cas9 is used in gene editing.","A guide RNA matches the target DNA sequence|guide rna|grna|target","Cas9 cuts the DNA there so genes can be removed, changed or inserted|cas9|cut|insert|remove|edit"],
["D2.1","Distinguish",3,"Distinguish between mitosis and meiosis.","Mitosis produces two cells; meiosis produces four|two|four","Mitosis gives identical diploid cells; meiosis gives haploid cells|diploid|haploid|identical","Meiosis creates variation by crossing over and independent assortment|variation|crossing over|independent assortment|different"],
["D2.1","State",2,"State two stages of mitosis.","Prophase|prophase","Metaphase|metaphase","Anaphase|anaphase","Telophase|telophase"],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how DNA methylation affects gene expression.","Methyl groups are added to DNA (usually cytosine)|methyl","This usually switches genes off / reduces transcription|switch off|silenc|reduce|less transcription|inhibit"],
["D2.2","Define",2,"Define epigenetics.","Heritable changes in gene expression|gene expression|heritable","Without any change to the DNA base sequence|without|no change|base sequence"],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to a plant cell placed in a hypotonic solution.","Water enters the cell by osmosis|water enters|water moves in|osmosis","The cell becomes turgid; the cell wall stops it bursting|turgid|swell|wall"],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to an animal cell placed in a hypertonic solution.","Water leaves the cell by osmosis|water leaves|water moves out|osmosis","The cell shrinks (becomes crenated)|shrink|crenat|shrivel"],
["D3.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between sexual and asexual reproduction.","Sexual: gametes from two parents fuse, so offspring are genetically varied|two parents|gametes|fertilis|fertiliz|varied|variation","Asexual: one parent, so offspring are genetically identical clones|one parent|single parent|identical|clone"],
["D3.1","State",2,"State two hormones involved in the menstrual cycle.","FSH|fsh|follicle stimulating","LH|lh|luteinising|luteinizing","Oestrogen|oestrogen|estrogen","Progesterone|progesterone"],
["D3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between genotype and phenotype.","Genotype: the alleles an organism has|allele|genetic makeup|genes","Phenotype: its observable characteristics|observable|characteristics|traits|appearance|physical"],
["D3.2","Define",2,"Define codominance.","Both alleles are expressed|both alleles|both expressed","In the phenotype of a heterozygote, e.g. blood group AB|heterozygo|ab blood|phenotype"],
["D3.3","Outline",3,"Outline how blood glucose is lowered after a meal.","Beta cells in the pancreas detect high glucose and secrete insulin|insulin|pancreas|beta","Insulin makes liver and muscle cells take up glucose|take up|absorb|uptake|liver|muscle","Glucose is converted to glycogen for storage|glycogen"],
["D3.3","Define",2,"Define homeostasis.","Keeping the internal environment constant|constant|stable|internal environment","Within narrow limits despite external changes, using negative feedback|limits|negative feedback|despite"],
["D4.1","Outline",3,"Outline natural selection.","There is variation within a population (from mutation)|variation|vary","Individuals with useful traits survive and reproduce more|survive|reproduce|advantage|better adapted|fittest","They pass on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations|pass on|allele|frequency|generations|offspring"],
["D4.1","Explain",2,"Explain how antibiotic resistance spreads in bacteria.","A random mutation makes some bacteria resistant|mutation|random","Antibiotics kill the others, so resistant bacteria survive and multiply|survive|reproduce|multiply|selection|kill"],
["D4.2","Define",2,"Define keystone species.","A species with a disproportionately large effect on its ecosystem|large effect|disproportionate|important","Whose removal would change the whole community|removal|collapse|change|ecosystem"],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline primary succession.","It starts on bare ground with no soil, e.g. new volcanic rock|bare|no soil|rock|volcan","Pioneer species colonise, soil forms and communities change until a climax community|pioneer|lichen|moss|soil|climax"],
["D4.3","Explain",2,"Explain how the enhanced greenhouse effect causes global warming.","Human activity increases greenhouse gases such as CO₂ and methane|human|greenhouse gas|carbon dioxide|co2|methane","These trap more of the infrared (long-wave) radiation emitted by the Earth|trap|infrared|longwave|long wave|radiation|heat"],
["D4.3","State",2,"State two effects of climate change on ecosystems.","Species ranges shift towards the poles or uphill|range|shift|poleward|higher altitude","Coral bleaching|coral","Loss of sea-ice habitat for polar species|ice|polar","Mismatched timing of events (phenology)|timing|phenolog","Extinctions|extinct"],
]);

/* ---------- more questions, each with a model answer and an examiner's tip ---------- */
QB("bio", [
["A1.1","Explain",2,"Explain how cohesion between water molecules helps water move up the xylem.","Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules together|hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds","So water is pulled up as a continuous column under tension as water evaporates from leaves|continuous column|column|pulled|tension|transpiration",{model:"Hydrogen bonds hold water molecules together, so when water evaporates from the leaves, the continuous column of water in the xylem is pulled up under tension.",tip:"Name the bond (hydrogen) and the effect (a continuous column pulled up)."}],
["A1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why ice floats on liquid water.","Ice is less dense than liquid water|less dense|lower density|density","Hydrogen bonds hold the molecules further apart in an open lattice|lattice|further apart|spaced out|hydrogen bond",{model:"When water freezes, hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open lattice, further apart than in liquid water, so ice is less dense and floats.",tip:"Density is the key word; the lattice explains it."}],
["A1.2","Outline",3,"Outline the structure of a DNA nucleotide.","A phosphate group|phosphate","A deoxyribose sugar|deoxyribose|pentose","A nitrogenous base: adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine|nitrogenous base|base|adenine|thymine|guanine|cytosine",{model:"A DNA nucleotide is made of a phosphate group, a deoxyribose sugar and a nitrogenous base (adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine).",tip:"Three marks, three parts: phosphate, deoxyribose, base."}],
["A1.2","State",2,"State how the two strands of a DNA molecule are held together.","By hydrogen bonds|hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonds","Between complementary base pairs, A with T and C with G|complementary|base pairs|base pairing|adenine",{model:"The two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: adenine with thymine and cytosine with guanine.",tip:"Mention both the bond type and the base pairing."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the Miller–Urey experiment.","It simulated the early Earth's atmosphere (methane, ammonia, hydrogen, water) with electric sparks as lightning|methane|ammonia|sparks|lightning|electric","Amino acids and other organic molecules formed from inorganic ones|amino acids|organic",{model:"Miller and Urey passed electric sparks, simulating lightning, through a mixture of methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapour like the early atmosphere. Amino acids and other organic molecules formed.",tip:"Describe the set-up and the result."}],
["A2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the RNA world hypothesis.","RNA was the first genetic material, before DNA|first|before dna|earliest|genetic material","Because RNA can both store information and catalyse reactions (ribozymes)|catalyse|catalyze|ribozyme|ribozymes|enzyme",{model:"The hypothesis is that RNA was the first genetic material, before DNA and proteins, because RNA can both store information and act as a catalyst (ribozymes).",tip:"The double role of RNA (information and catalysis) is the core argument."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline the functions of the cell wall in plant cells.","It provides support and strength, keeping the cell's shape|support|strength|shape|rigid","It stops the cell bursting by resisting turgor pressure|bursting|burst|turgor|pressure",{model:"The cellulose cell wall supports the cell and keeps its shape, and it resists turgor pressure so the cell does not burst when water enters.",tip:"Give two functions, one sentence each."}],
["A2.2","Outline",2,"Outline why electron microscopes show more detail than light microscopes.","Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light|wavelength","So they give a higher resolution|resolution|resolving",{model:"Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light, so electron microscopes have a much higher resolution and can separate smaller structures.",tip:"Detail means resolution, not magnification."}],
["A2.3","State",2,"State two structural features common to all viruses.","Genetic material, either DNA or RNA|genetic material|dna|rna|nucleic acid","A protein coat (capsid)|capsid|protein coat|coat",{model:"Every virus has genetic material (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat called a capsid.",tip:"Avoid features only some viruses have, such as an envelope."}],
["A2.3","Explain",2,"Explain why antibiotics are not effective against viruses.","Antibiotics target bacterial structures or processes, such as cell walls or bacterial ribosomes|cell wall|cell walls|bacterial|ribosomes","Viruses lack these and use the host cell's machinery|host cell|host|lack|no metabolism",{model:"Antibiotics work by attacking structures and processes in bacteria, such as cell walls or 70S ribosomes. Viruses have none of these: they reproduce using the host cell's machinery.",tip:"Explain what antibiotics target, then why viruses don't have it."}],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline one difficulty with applying the biological species concept.","It cannot be used for organisms that reproduce asexually, such as bacteria|asexual|asexually|bacteria","Or for fossils, or populations that never meet or form hybrids|fossils|fossil|extinct|never meet|hybrids",{model:"The concept depends on interbreeding, so it cannot be used for organisms that reproduce asexually, such as bacteria, or for extinct species known only as fossils.",tip:"Link the difficulty to the definition: it relies on interbreeding."}],
["A3.1","Outline",2,"Outline what a dichotomous key is used for.","To identify an organism|identify|identification","By working through a series of paired choices about its features|paired|two choices|pairs|series|features|characteristics",{model:"A dichotomous key is used to identify an organism by working through a series of paired choices about its visible features.",tip:"Say what it does (identifies) and how (paired choices)."}],
["A3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how molecular data are used to construct a cladogram.","Base or amino acid sequences of species are compared|compared|compare|base sequence|amino acid sequence","The fewer the differences, the more recent the common ancestor, so they are placed closer together|fewer differences|common ancestor|more recent|closely related",{model:"The base sequences of a gene (or the amino acid sequences of a protein) are compared between species. Species with fewer differences share a more recent common ancestor, so they branch closer together.",tip:"Connect the number of differences to how recently the species diverged."}],
["A3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between homologous and analogous structures.","Homologous structures share a common ancestry and basic structure, even if their functions differ|common ancestor|ancestry|same basic structure|pentadactyl","Analogous structures have the same function but evolved separately (convergent evolution)|same function|convergent|evolved separately|independently",{model:"Homologous structures, such as the pentadactyl limb, share a common ancestry and basic structure but can have different functions. Analogous structures, such as bird and insect wings, do the same job but evolved separately.",tip:"An example of each makes the difference clear."}],
["A4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how selective breeding provides evidence for evolution.","Humans choose individuals with desired traits to breed (artificial selection)|humans|artificial|desired|chosen|choose","Large changes appear in a species over relatively few generations, showing species can change|generations|large changes|changed|short time|differ",{model:"In artificial selection, humans choose individuals with desired traits to breed. Breeds such as dogs changed greatly in a few hundred generations, showing that species can change, as evolution proposes.",tip:"Make the link: if humans can change a species quickly, natural selection can over longer times."}],
["A4.1","Define",2,"Define the term speciation.","The formation of a new species|new species|formation","When populations become reproductively isolated and diverge|isolated|isolation|diverge|interbreed",{model:"Speciation is the formation of a new species, when populations become reproductively isolated and diverge until they can no longer interbreed.",tip:"Include reproductive isolation: it is what makes a new species."}],
["A4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how rewilding can help restore biodiversity.","Missing species, such as top predators, are reintroduced or land is left to return to a natural state|reintroduce|reintroduced|reintroduction|predators|natural state","This restores food webs and natural processes so more species can live there|food web|food webs|natural processes|more species|ecosystem",{model:"Rewilding reintroduces missing species, such as wolves or beavers, and lets land return to a natural state. This restores food webs and natural processes, so more species can survive there.",tip:"A named example such as wolves in Yellowstone earns credibility."}],
["A4.2","Explain",2,"Explain why habitat loss reduces biodiversity.","Species lose their food, shelter or breeding sites|food|shelter|breeding|resources","So their populations shrink, become fragmented or go extinct|extinct|extinction|shrink|fragmented|decline",{model:"When a habitat is destroyed, the species living there lose their food, shelter and breeding sites, so their populations decline, become fragmented or go extinct.",tip:"Cause, then consequence for populations."}],
["B1.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between amylose and cellulose.","Amylose is made of alpha-glucose; cellulose of beta-glucose|alpha|beta","Amylose is coiled (helical) for storage; cellulose forms straight chains bundled into fibres for structure|coiled|helix|helical|straight|fibres|fibers",{model:"Amylose is a polymer of alpha-glucose that coils into a compact helix for energy storage. Cellulose is a polymer of beta-glucose that forms straight chains bundled into strong fibres for structure.",tip:"Monomer, shape and function: compare all three."}],
["B1.1","Outline",2,"Outline why triglycerides are used for long-term energy storage.","They release about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates|twice|per gram|energy dense|more energy","They are insoluble, so they don't affect osmosis, and are light for the energy stored|insoluble|osmosis|light|lighter|compact",{model:"Triglycerides release about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates, and they are insoluble, so large stores do not affect osmosis and add little mass.",tip:"Energy per gram is the mark examiners always look for."}],
["B1.2","Outline",2,"Outline how a peptide bond forms.","By a condensation reaction|condensation","Between the amine group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another, releasing water|carboxyl|amine|amino group|water|cooh",{model:"A peptide bond forms in a condensation reaction between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amine group of another, releasing a molecule of water.",tip:"Name the reaction type and the two groups involved."}],
["B1.2","Explain",2,"Explain how the shape of a protein depends on its amino acid sequence.","The sequence determines where bonds form between R groups (hydrogen, ionic, disulfide)|bonds|interactions|hydrogen|disulfide|ionic|r groups","Which fold the chain into a specific 3D (tertiary) shape|fold|folding|tertiary|3d|three dimensional",{model:"The amino acid sequence decides which R groups are next to each other, and so where hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and disulfide bridges form. These fold the chain into its specific 3D tertiary shape.",tip:"Go from sequence → bonds → folding → shape."}],
["B2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of cholesterol in animal cell membranes.","It regulates the fluidity of the membrane|fluidity|fluid","It keeps the membrane stable and reduces its permeability to small ions|stable|stability|permeability|rigid",{model:"Cholesterol sits between the phospholipids and regulates membrane fluidity, keeping it stable across temperatures and reducing its permeability to small ions.",tip:"Fluidity is the key word."}],
["B2.1","Explain",2,"Explain why phospholipids form a bilayer in water.","Phospholipids have hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails|hydrophilic|hydrophobic","The heads face the water on each side, while the tails face inwards, away from water|face|inwards|inward|away from water|tails",{model:"Phospholipids have hydrophilic phosphate heads and hydrophobic fatty acid tails. In water they form a bilayer with the heads facing the water on both sides and the tails pointing inwards, away from it.",tip:"Use the words hydrophilic and hydrophobic."}],
["B2.2","State",2,"State the functions of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.","Rough ER: proteins are made on its ribosomes, often for secretion|synthesis|synthesise|synthesize|make proteins|ribosomes","Golgi apparatus: modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles|modifies|packages|packaging|vesicles|sorts",{model:"The rough ER makes proteins on its ribosomes, often for secretion. The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts and packages these proteins into vesicles.",tip:"One function for each organelle."}],
["B2.2","Outline",2,"Outline why the nucleus is separated from the cytoplasm by a nuclear envelope.","It keeps transcription separate from translation|transcription|translation","So mRNA can be processed (modified) before it is translated, and DNA is protected|processed|processing|modified|splicing|protect|protects",{model:"The nuclear envelope keeps transcription in the nucleus separate from translation in the cytoplasm, so mRNA can be processed before it is translated, and the DNA is protected.",tip:"Compartmentalisation: say which two processes it separates."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline how stem cells become specialized.","Some genes are switched on (expressed) and others switched off|genes|expressed|expression|switched","This differentiation gives the cell a specific structure and function|differentiation|differentiate|specific function",{model:"During differentiation, certain genes are expressed while others are switched off, so the cell develops the specific structure and function of one cell type.",tip:"Gene expression is the mechanism behind differentiation."}],
["B2.3","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of red blood cells for carrying oxygen.","No nucleus, leaving more room for haemoglobin|nucleus|room|space","Biconcave shape gives a large surface area for diffusion|biconcave|surface area","Full of haemoglobin, which binds oxygen|haemoglobin|hemoglobin","Small and flexible, to pass through capillaries|flexible|capillaries|small",{model:"Red blood cells have no nucleus, leaving more room for haemoglobin, and a biconcave shape that gives a large surface area for oxygen to diffuse in.",tip:"Pair each feature with how it helps."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of surfactant in the lungs.","It reduces surface tension in the alveoli|surface tension","Stopping the alveoli collapsing or sticking together|collapse|collapsing|sticking",{model:"Surfactant reduces the surface tension of the moisture lining the alveoli, which stops them collapsing and sticking together when we breathe out.",tip:"Two marks: what it reduces, and what that prevents."}],
["B3.1","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of leaves for gas exchange.","Stomata let gases in and out|stomata|stoma","Air spaces in the spongy mesophyll|air spaces|spongy","A thin, flat shape gives a large surface area and a short diffusion distance|thin|flat|surface area|short distance",{model:"Stomata let carbon dioxide in and oxygen out, and air spaces in the spongy mesophyll let gases diffuse to every cell.",tip:"Pick two clearly different features."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how the structure of capillaries suits their function.","Their walls are one cell thick, giving a short diffusion distance|one cell|thin|short diffusion","They have pores (fenestrations) that let tissue fluid form|pores|fenestrations|gaps|leaky","They are narrow and highly branched, giving a large surface area|narrow|surface area|branched|network",{model:"Capillary walls are one cell thick, so substances diffuse only a short distance, and the capillaries form a highly branched network with a large surface area for exchange.",tip:"Structure → function, for each feature."}],
["B3.2","Outline",2,"Outline how phloem transports sugars.","Sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem at the source|loaded|loading|source|active","Water follows by osmosis, raising the pressure, which pushes sap towards the sink (mass flow)|pressure|osmosis|mass flow|sink",{model:"Sucrose is actively loaded into the phloem at a source such as a leaf. Water follows by osmosis, raising the pressure, which pushes the sap by mass flow to a sink such as a root.",tip:"Use the terms source, sink and mass flow."}],
["B3.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of ATP in muscle contraction.","ATP binding makes the myosin head detach from actin|detach|release|unbind|breaks","Hydrolysis of ATP re-cocks the myosin head so it can bind again and pull|hydrolysis|cocks|cocked|energy|power stroke|reset",{model:"ATP binds to the myosin head, making it detach from actin. Hydrolysis of the ATP then re-cocks the head so it can bind further along the actin and pull again.",tip:"Two steps: detaching, then re-cocking."}],
["B3.3","Explain",2,"Explain why skeletal muscles work in antagonistic pairs.","Muscles can only pull by contracting; they cannot push|only pull|cannot push|pull","So one muscle contracts while the other relaxes, moving the bone back, e.g. biceps and triceps|relaxes|biceps|triceps|other muscle|opposite",{model:"Muscles can only pull, so a joint needs one muscle to bend it and another to straighten it: when the biceps contracts, the triceps relaxes, and the reverse.",tip:"An example pair such as biceps and triceps secures the mark."}],
["B4.1","Outline",2,"Outline two adaptations of hydrophytes (aquatic plants).","Air spaces (aerenchyma) for buoyancy and oxygen|air spaces|aerenchyma|buoyancy|float","Stomata on the upper surface of floating leaves|upper surface|stomata","A thin cuticle or none|cuticle","Large, flat floating leaves to catch light|large leaves|floating leaves|flat",{model:"Hydrophytes have air spaces (aerenchyma) that keep them afloat and store oxygen, and their floating leaves have stomata on the upper surface.",tip:"Contrast with xerophytes if it helps you remember."}],
["B4.1","Define",2,"Define the term abiotic factor and give one example.","A non-living feature of the environment|non living|nonliving|physical|chemical","For example temperature, light, water, pH or salinity|temperature|light|water|ph|salinity|rainfall",{model:"An abiotic factor is a non-living feature of the environment, such as temperature, light intensity or soil pH.",tip:"Definition plus example: the question asks for both."}],
["B4.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between autotrophs and heterotrophs.","Autotrophs make their own organic molecules from inorganic substances, e.g. by photosynthesis|own food|own organic|photosynthesis|inorganic|make their own","Heterotrophs obtain organic molecules from other organisms|other organisms|eat|consume|obtain",{model:"Autotrophs make their own organic molecules from inorganic substances, for example by photosynthesis. Heterotrophs obtain organic molecules by consuming other organisms.",tip:"Source of organic molecules is the difference."}],
["B4.2","Outline",2,"Outline what is meant by an ecological niche.","The role a species plays in its ecosystem|role","Including its habitat, interactions with other species and the resources it uses|habitat|interactions|resources|food|feeding",{model:"A niche is the role a species plays in its ecosystem: where it lives, what it feeds on, how it interacts with other species and which resources it uses.",tip:"A niche is more than a habitat: include its role and interactions."}],
["C1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the induced-fit model of enzyme action.","The active site changes shape slightly as the substrate binds|changes shape|alters|adjusts|moulds","To fit the substrate more closely, putting strain on its bonds|strain|stress|weaken|tightly|closely",{model:"When the substrate binds, the active site changes shape slightly to fit it more closely, which strains the substrate's bonds and lowers the activation energy.",tip:"Contrast it with the rigid lock-and-key model if asked."}],
["C1.1","Explain",2,"Explain the effect of increasing substrate concentration on the rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction.","The rate rises because enzyme–substrate collisions become more frequent|collisions|more frequent|faster","Until all active sites are occupied (saturation), when the rate levels off|saturated|saturation|all active sites|plateau|levels off",{model:"At first the rate rises because more frequent collisions form more enzyme–substrate complexes. Once all active sites are occupied, the enzyme is saturated and the rate levels off.",tip:"Explain both parts of the curve: rising and levelling off."}],
["C1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of oxygen in aerobic respiration.","It is the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain|final electron acceptor|electron acceptor|accepts electrons","It combines with electrons and protons to form water, so the chain and ATP production can continue|water|continue|keeps going|h2o",{model:"Oxygen is the final electron acceptor at the end of the electron transport chain. It combines with electrons and protons to form water, which keeps the chain, and ATP production, going.",tip:"'Final electron acceptor' is the phrase markschemes use."}],
["C1.2","State",2,"State the products of anaerobic respiration in yeast.","Ethanol|ethanol|alcohol","Carbon dioxide|carbon dioxide|co2",{model:"Ethanol and carbon dioxide (with a small amount of ATP).",tip:"Don't confuse yeast (ethanol and CO2) with humans (lactate)."}],
["C1.3","Outline",2,"Outline the role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis.","It absorbs light energy, mainly red and blue light|absorbs|absorb|light","Excited electrons are passed on, converting light energy into chemical energy|electrons|excited|chemical energy|converts",{model:"Chlorophyll absorbs light, mainly red and blue wavelengths. The energy excites electrons, which are passed along so the light energy is converted into chemical energy.",tip:"Say what it absorbs and what happens to the energy."}],
["C1.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between the light-dependent and light-independent reactions of photosynthesis.","The light-dependent reactions happen in the thylakoids and produce ATP and NADPH, releasing oxygen|thylakoid|thylakoids|nadph|oxygen","The light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) happen in the stroma and fix carbon dioxide into sugars|stroma|calvin|fixes|fixed|carbon dioxide|sugars",{model:"The light-dependent reactions take place in the thylakoids, using light to make ATP and NADPH and release oxygen. The Calvin cycle takes place in the stroma and uses them to fix carbon dioxide into sugars.",tip:"Location and products for each stage."}],
["C2.1","Define",2,"Define the term ligand and give an example.","A signalling molecule that binds specifically to a receptor|binds|receptor|signalling molecule","For example a hormone, neurotransmitter or cytokine|hormone|neurotransmitter|cytokine|insulin|adrenaline",{model:"A ligand is a signalling molecule that binds specifically to a receptor, for example the hormone insulin or the neurotransmitter acetylcholine.",tip:"Definition plus a named example."}],
["C2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of a second messenger such as cyclic AMP.","A hormone binding to a membrane receptor triggers production of cAMP inside the cell|inside|triggers|production|membrane receptor","cAMP activates enzymes in a cascade that amplifies the response|activates|cascade|amplify|amplifies|amplification|enzymes",{model:"When a hormone such as adrenaline binds to a receptor on the membrane, cAMP is produced inside the cell. cAMP activates a cascade of enzymes that amplifies the response.",tip:"The hormone stays outside; the second messenger carries the signal inside."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how myelination increases the speed of nerve impulses.","Myelin insulates the axon|insulates|insulation|insulating","So the impulse jumps between the nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction)|nodes|ranvier|saltatory|jumps",{model:"Myelin insulates the axon, so depolarisation happens only at the gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, and the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction).",tip:"Use the term saltatory conduction."}],
["C2.2","Outline",2,"Outline depolarization during an action potential.","Voltage-gated sodium channels open|sodium channels|sodium|na","Sodium ions diffuse in, so the inside of the axon becomes positive|diffuse in|rush in|positive|inside",{model:"Voltage-gated sodium channels open, sodium ions diffuse into the axon, and the inside becomes positive compared with the outside.",tip:"Name the ion and its direction."}],
["C3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of the hypothalamus in linking the nervous and endocrine systems.","It receives nerve signals and monitors conditions in the body|receives|monitors|detects|senses","It controls the pituitary gland, which releases hormones|pituitary",{model:"The hypothalamus monitors the body through nerve signals and blood, and it controls the pituitary gland, which releases hormones.",tip:"The pituitary is the link to the endocrine system."}],
["C3.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a voluntary action and a reflex action.","A voluntary action is a deliberate decision controlled by the brain's cortex|cortex|brain|decision|deliberate|chooses","A reflex is a rapid, automatic response that does not involve conscious thought, often via the spinal cord|automatic|involuntary|unconscious|spinal|rapid",{model:"A voluntary action is a deliberate decision controlled by the cerebral cortex. A reflex is a rapid, automatic response that bypasses conscious thought, often through the spinal cord.",tip:"Contrast control: decided vs automatic."}],
["C3.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of phagocytes in defence against disease.","They engulf pathogens|engulf|engulfs|ingest|surround","And digest them with enzymes from lysosomes|digest|digests|lysosomes|enzymes|destroy",{model:"Phagocytes engulf pathogens into a vesicle and digest them with enzymes from lysosomes.",tip:"Engulf, then digest: two marks."}],
["C3.2","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between antigens and antibodies.","Antigens are molecules on a pathogen's surface that trigger an immune response|surface|trigger|foreign|recognised","Antibodies are proteins made by plasma cells (B cells) that bind to specific antigens|proteins|plasma cells|b cells|lymphocytes|bind",{model:"Antigens are molecules on the surface of a pathogen that trigger an immune response. Antibodies are proteins made by plasma cells that bind to a specific antigen.",tip:"Say what each one is and where it comes from."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline the shape of a sigmoid population growth curve.","Slow growth at first, then rapid, exponential growth|exponential|rapid|lag","Growth then slows as limiting factors act, levelling off at the carrying capacity|carrying capacity|levels off|plateau|limiting factors",{model:"A sigmoid curve starts with slow growth, then rapid exponential growth, then slows as limiting factors take effect, levelling off at the carrying capacity.",tip:"Name the three phases and the carrying capacity."}],
["C4.1","Outline",2,"Outline how random quadrat sampling estimates the population size of a plant.","Quadrats are placed at coordinates chosen from random numbers|coordinates|random numbers|grid|placed","The mean number per quadrat is multiplied up to the total area|mean|average|multiply|multiplied|total area|scale",{model:"Quadrats are placed at coordinates chosen using random numbers, the plants in each are counted, and the mean per quadrat is multiplied by the number of quadrats that fit in the whole area.",tip:"Say how positions are chosen and how the estimate is calculated."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline why energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient.","Energy is lost as heat from respiration|heat|respiration","Not all of an organism is eaten or digested; some is lost in faeces or dead remains|not eaten|faeces|feces|undigested|excretion|bones",{model:"Much of the energy is lost as heat from respiration, and not all of each organism is eaten or digested, so some energy is lost in faeces and dead remains.",tip:"Give two separate losses."}],
["C4.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of decomposers in the carbon cycle.","They break down dead organic matter|break down|dead|rot","Releasing carbon dioxide into the air through respiration|carbon dioxide|co2|respiration|release",{model:"Decomposers break down dead organic matter and, by respiring, release the carbon in it back into the air as carbon dioxide.",tip:"Link breakdown to the CO2 released by respiration."}],
["D1.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of DNA ligase in replication.","It joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand|okazaki|lagging|fragments","By forming phosphodiester bonds in the sugar–phosphate backbone|phosphodiester|backbone|seals",{model:"DNA ligase joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand by forming phosphodiester bonds, sealing the sugar–phosphate backbone.",tip:"Name the fragments and the bond."}],
["D1.1","Explain",2,"Explain why replication is continuous on one strand but discontinuous on the other.","DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides in the 5′ to 3′ direction|5 to 3|five to three|direction","The strands are antiparallel, so the lagging strand is made in short (Okazaki) fragments|antiparallel|okazaki|fragments",{model:"DNA polymerase only adds nucleotides in the 5′ to 3′ direction. Because the strands are antiparallel, one strand is made continuously, while the lagging strand is made in short Okazaki fragments.",tip:"Two ideas: the direction rule and antiparallel strands."}],
["D1.2","Outline",2,"Outline the role of tRNA in translation.","Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome|carries|brings|amino acid","Its anticodon pairs with the complementary codon on the mRNA|anticodon|codon|complementary",{model:"Each tRNA carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome, where its anticodon pairs with the complementary mRNA codon, so amino acids are added in the right order.",tip:"Codon and anticodon are the key terms."}],
["D1.2","Explain",2,"Explain why the genetic code is described as degenerate.","More than one codon can code for the same amino acid|more than one|same amino acid|several codons|multiple","Because there are 64 codons but only 20 amino acids|64|20",{model:"The code is degenerate because several codons can code for the same amino acid: there are 64 codons but only 20 amino acids.",tip:"Quote the numbers 64 and 20."}],
["D1.3","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between a substitution mutation and a frameshift mutation.","A substitution replaces one base, changing at most one codon or amino acid|replaces|one base|single base|one codon|one amino acid","A frameshift (an insertion or deletion) shifts the reading frame, changing every codon after it|insertion|deletion|reading frame|every codon|all codons",{model:"A substitution replaces one base with another, so at most one codon and one amino acid change. A frameshift, caused by inserting or deleting a base, shifts the reading frame, changing every codon after it.",tip:"Compare how much of the protein is affected."}],
["D1.3","Outline",2,"Outline one cause of gene mutations.","A named mutagen such as UV light, X-rays or chemicals in tobacco smoke|uv|ultraviolet|x rays|radiation|chemicals|mutagen|mutagens|carcinogen","It damages DNA or changes its base sequence|damage|damages|alters|changes the base|base sequence",{model:"Mutagens such as ultraviolet radiation damage DNA and can change its base sequence, for example by causing errors when the DNA is copied.",tip:"Name the cause, then say what it does to DNA."}],
["D2.1","Outline",2,"Outline the role of crossing over in meiosis.","Homologous chromosomes exchange sections of chromatids at chiasmata in prophase I|exchange|swap|chiasma|chiasmata|homologous","Producing new combinations of alleles, which increases genetic variation|variation|new combinations|recombination|different combinations",{model:"In prophase I, homologous chromosomes exchange sections of their chromatids at chiasmata, producing new combinations of alleles and so more genetic variation.",tip:"Say when it happens and what it produces."}],
["D2.1","State",2,"State what happens during anaphase of mitosis.","Sister chromatids separate as the centromeres split|chromatids|separate|split","They are pulled to opposite poles by the spindle fibres|poles|spindle|opposite ends|pulled",{model:"In anaphase the centromeres split, the sister chromatids separate, and spindle fibres pull them to opposite poles of the cell.",tip:"Two marks: separation and movement to the poles."}],
["D2.2","Outline",2,"Outline how transcription factors regulate gene expression.","They are proteins that bind to the promoter or other specific DNA sequences|bind|binding|promoter|dna","They increase or decrease the rate at which RNA polymerase transcribes the gene|increase|decrease|activate|repress|rna polymerase|switch on",{model:"Transcription factors are proteins that bind to specific DNA sequences, such as the promoter, and increase or decrease the rate at which RNA polymerase transcribes the gene.",tip:"Where they bind and what they change."}],
["D2.2","Explain",2,"Explain how identical twins can develop different phenotypes.","They have the same DNA (genotype)|same dna|same genotype|same genes","But different environments cause different epigenetic changes, such as methylation, altering gene expression|environment|environments|epigenetic|methylation|lifestyle|diet",{model:"Identical twins have the same genotype, but different environments and lifestyles cause different epigenetic changes, such as DNA methylation, so different genes are expressed.",tip:"Genotype is the same; the difference is in gene expression."}],
["D2.3","Define",2,"Define water potential.","The potential energy of water, its tendency to move|potential energy|tendency|free energy","Water moves from higher to lower water potential|higher to lower|high to low|lower","It is measured in kPa, and pure water is 0|kpa|kilopascal|zero",{model:"Water potential is the potential energy of water, its tendency to move from one place to another. Water moves from higher to lower water potential; it is measured in kPa, and pure water is 0.",tip:"Include the direction water moves."}],
["D2.3","Outline",2,"Outline what happens to a plant cell placed in a hypertonic solution.","Water leaves the cell by osmosis|leaves|osmosis|out of the cell","The cytoplasm shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the cell wall (plasmolysis)|plasmolysis|plasmolysed|pulls away|shrinks|flaccid",{model:"Water leaves the cell by osmosis, so the cytoplasm shrinks and the membrane pulls away from the cell wall: the cell becomes plasmolysed.",tip:"Use the term plasmolysis."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline the roles of FSH and LH in the menstrual cycle.","FSH stimulates the development of a follicle, which secretes oestrogen|follicle|follicles|oestrogen|estrogen","An LH surge triggers ovulation and the formation of the corpus luteum|ovulation|corpus luteum|surge",{model:"FSH stimulates a follicle to develop and secrete oestrogen. A surge in LH then triggers ovulation and turns the follicle into the corpus luteum.",tip:"One role per hormone."}],
["D3.1","Outline",2,"Outline two differences between male and female gametes in humans.","Sperm are small; eggs are large|small|large|size|larger","Sperm can swim using a flagellum; eggs cannot move by themselves|motile|flagellum|tail|swim","Eggs contain food reserves in their cytoplasm; sperm have very little|food|yolk|cytoplasm|nutrients","Millions of sperm are made but few eggs|millions|many|few|number",{model:"Sperm are tiny and swim using a flagellum, while eggs are large, cannot move by themselves and contain food reserves.",tip:"State both sides of each difference."}],
["D3.2","Outline",2,"Outline why sex-linked conditions such as haemophilia are more common in males.","The recessive allele is carried on the X chromosome|x chromosome|on the x|x linked","Males have only one X, so one recessive allele causes the condition, while females need two copies|only one|one x|single x|xy|two copies|need two",{model:"The recessive allele is on the X chromosome. Males have only one X, so one copy of the allele causes haemophilia, while females need two copies and are usually carriers.",tip:"Explain the difference in X chromosomes between the sexes."}],
["D3.2","Define",2,"Define the term allele.","One of the different forms of a gene|version|form|variant","Found at the same locus; alleles differ in their base sequence|locus|same position|base sequence|differ",{model:"An allele is one of the different forms of a gene, found at the same locus on a chromosome and differing slightly in base sequence.",tip:"Don't confuse allele with gene."}],
["D3.3","Outline",2,"Outline how the body responds when core temperature rises.","Skin arterioles dilate (vasodilation), increasing heat loss|vasodilation|dilate|dilation|blood flow","Sweat is released and cools the skin by evaporation|sweat|sweating|evaporation","Hair erector muscles relax so hairs lie flat|flat|relax",{model:"Arterioles in the skin dilate, so more blood flows near the surface and loses heat, and sweat glands release sweat that cools the skin as it evaporates.",tip:"Pick two responses and say how each loses heat."}],
["D3.3","Explain",2,"Explain the role of negative feedback in homeostasis.","A change from the set point is detected by receptors|set point|detected|receptors|deviation","A response then reverses the change, returning the value to the set point|reverses|opposes|returns|counteracts|corrects",{model:"When a variable moves away from its set point, receptors detect the change, and effectors bring about a response that reverses it, returning the value to the set point.",tip:"Use 'set point' and 'reverses the change'."}],
["D4.1","Outline",2,"Outline why variation is necessary for natural selection.","Individuals with favourable variations are more likely to survive and reproduce|survive|reproduce|favourable|advantage|fitter","They pass on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations|pass on|alleles|offspring|frequency|frequencies",{model:"Without variation there is nothing to select. Individuals with favourable variations survive and reproduce more, passing on their alleles, so allele frequencies change over generations.",tip:"Natural selection acts on existing variation; make that explicit."}],
["D4.1","Distinguish",2,"Distinguish between directional and stabilizing selection.","Directional selection favours one extreme phenotype, shifting the mean|one extreme|extreme|shifts|shift","Stabilizing selection favours the intermediate phenotype, reducing variation|intermediate|average|middle|reduces variation|narrows",{model:"Directional selection favours one extreme phenotype, so the mean shifts in that direction. Stabilizing selection favours the intermediate phenotype, reducing variation around the mean.",tip:"Describe what happens to the mean and the spread."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline how eutrophication can harm aquatic ecosystems.","Excess nutrients such as nitrates from fertilisers cause algal blooms|nutrients|nitrates|phosphates|fertiliser|fertilizer|algal bloom|algae","Bacteria decomposing the dead algae use up oxygen, so fish and other animals die|oxygen|decomposers|bacteria|fish die|die",{model:"Fertiliser run-off adds nitrates and phosphates, causing algal blooms. When the algae die, bacteria decompose them and use up the oxygen, so fish and other animals die.",tip:"Follow the chain: nutrients → algae → decomposition → low oxygen."}],
["D4.2","Outline",2,"Outline two features of a stable ecosystem.","A supply of energy, usually sunlight|sunlight|sun|energy supply","Nutrients are recycled|recycling|recycled|nutrient cycling","High biodiversity and genetic diversity|biodiversity|diversity","Climate variables stay within the tolerance of its species|climate|tolerance",{model:"A stable ecosystem has a constant supply of energy from sunlight, and its nutrients are recycled by decomposers.",tip:"Two separate features, one sentence each."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how the melting of polar ice can cause further warming.","Ice reflects sunlight (high albedo), while open water absorbs more|albedo|reflects|reflect|absorbs|absorb","So more heat is absorbed, which melts more ice, and the cycle continues|more ice|melts more|more melting|cycle|continues",{model:"Ice reflects most sunlight (high albedo). As it melts, darker ocean absorbs more heat, which warms the water and melts more ice, so the warming feeds itself.",tip:"Albedo is the key term; then show the loop."}],
["D4.3","Outline",2,"Outline how carbon sequestration helps reduce global warming.","Carbon dioxide is captured and stored, e.g. by planting forests or storing it underground|forests|trees|planting|underground|stored|captured","So less carbon dioxide stays in the air to add to the greenhouse effect|removed|greenhouse|less carbon dioxide|reduces",{model:"Carbon sequestration captures carbon dioxide and stores it, for example in new forests or rock underground, so less of it stays in the air to enhance the greenhouse effect.",tip:"Give an example of how carbon is stored."}],
]);
